//! lotus/stereo_limit.rye -- ALES158: the brickwall limiter carried into stereo, the ratio->∞ corner of the linked gain -- both a true ceiling and an image held whole. ALES49 named `limit`: a hard ceiling the signal may
//! touch yet never cross -- a sample within the ceiling passes byte for byte, a sample louder than it is pinned to sign(x)-ceil, the honest floor of the whole dynamics family. That is a mono edit, pinning each sample
//! independently. Carried into stereo, this rung is the compressor's linked gain (ALES157) taken to its LIMIT: the ratio->∞ corner already foreshadowed by ALES157's seat-at-threshold knob law.
//!
//! Lift the mono limiter per channel and an asymmetric transient -- loud on the left, quiet on the right -- would be flattened on the left while the right passes, shifting the stereo image. The honest stereo limiter takes
//! ALES157's shape to its limit: ONE gain from the linked detector, applied to both. The quiet beauty is that the linked form is SIMULTANEOUSLY a true brickwall (after the call no sample in either channel exceeds the
//! ceiling, since the peak lands on the ceiling and everything else is scaled below it) AND image-preserving (both channels share one gain, so the L:R ratio holds). The independent mono limiter gives only the first.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: refuse BadCeiling (a ceiling outside [1, sample_max]) and BadRange (a span past the shared length) BEFORE any write -- the checks are identical for both channels, so a refusal never limits one
//! channel and leaves the other untouched. Otherwise walk the span one sample-PAIR at a time: key = max(|left|, |right|), the louder of the two. key <= ceil leaves the pair within the ceiling -- both pass byte for byte.
//! key > ceil scales BOTH channels by the single fraction ceil/key: x' = sign(x)-(|x|-ceil/key). The peak-holding channel lands EXACTLY on ceil (key-ceil/key = ceil); the quieter channel is scaled by the identical
//! fraction, the L:R ratio kept.
//!
//! The crux -- five laws. THE BRICKWALL + LINKED-GAIN LAW (the stereo crux): after the call no sample in either channel exceeds ceil, and the peak-holding channel equals ALES49's mono limit byte for byte (a master where
//! the left always holds the peak has left' == mono limit(left)) while both channels are scaled by the same fraction ceil/key -- the independent lift (mono limit of the quiet right alone) genuinely differs. THE IMAGE LAW:
//! the shared gain preserves the L:R ratio -- an out-of-phase over-ceiling pair returns still out of phase, no magnitude expands, no sign flips. THE SUB-CEILING LAW: where the linked key is within the ceiling, both pass
//! byte for byte. THE CEILING-AT-MAX LAW: a ceiling at sample_max is the identity for all in-range audio. THE BALANCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: left.len == right.len after, a refusal leaving both channels untouched
//! and balanced, count = 0 the identity on both.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES158.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-063222_fill-ales158-lotus-stereo-limit.md
//! Stands on: lotus/limit.rye (ALES49) -- the mono brickwall limiter, its `limit` and `LimitError` reused whole (the peak-channel proof and the fault set); lotus/stereo_compress.rye (ALES157) -- the linked-gain sibling
//! whose ratio->∞ corner this rung is; lotus/stereo_gate.rye (ALES156) -- the rung that opened the stereo dynamics family with the linked detector; lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) -- the StereoClip and its balance invariant;
//! lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) -- the Clip, its buf/len/samples, and sample_max.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE -- software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) on one bench, siloed to lotus/. It changes sample VALUES only -- one comparison of the linked key and at most one multiply-
//! then-divide per channel per sample (in i64, |sample|-ceil <= 32767-32767 never overflowing), memoryless (no attack/release time base; that is a later rung), never a length. The ceiling is a magnitude in sample units,
//! not decibels. Nothing on the audio path can overflow -- ceil <= key on the scaled path, so every scaled magnitude is at most its input's, which already fits i16. No real sample rate, no anti-aliasing, no network, no
//! keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_limit.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const limit = @import("limit.rye");

pub const stereo_limit_version = "20260815.063222";

/// Stereo limiting's faults are exactly ALES49's own `LimitError` -- `BadCeiling` (a ceiling outside [1, sample_max]) and `BadRange` (a span past the samples) -- reused by name, since the linked lift adds no new fault: it
/// validates one shared ceiling and one span, then computes one gain per pair from both channels together.
pub const StereoLimitError = limit.LimitError;

/// Limit `count` sample-pairs starting at `start` in the stereo master `sc` to the magnitude `ceil`, with ONE LINKED GAIN across the two channels -- the ratio->∞ corner of ALES157's compressor. For each index the detector
/// key is `max(|left|, |right|)`: within the ceiling the pair passes byte for byte; above it BOTH channels are scaled by the single fraction `ceil/key`, so the peak-holding channel lands exactly on `ceil` and the quieter
/// one is scaled by the identical fraction -- a true brickwall (no written sample exceeds `ceil`) that also preserves the L:R ratio. After the call every written magnitude is at most `ceil` and no sign is changed.
/// Validates the ceiling and span ONCE before any write -- a refusal leaves both channels untouched and balanced. Values only -- never the length.
pub fn stereo_limit(sc: *pan.StereoClip, start: u32, count: u32, ceil: i32) StereoLimitError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced -- the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the linked gain preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);

    // invariant: the ceiling is a legal magnitude -- at least one sample unit (a zero ceiling would silence, not limit) and no greater than the i16 rail. Refused before any write, mirroring ALES49 exactly.
    if (ceil < 1) return error.BadCeiling;
    if (ceil > @as(i32, timeline.sample_max)) return error.BadCeiling;
    // invariant: the span lies wholly within the SHARED length -- checked once, without underflow, so it holds identically for both channels and a refusal never limits one channel and leaves the other torn.
    const len: u32 = sc.left.len;
    if (start > len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > len - start) return error.BadRange;

    const ceiling: i64 = @as(i64, ceil); // safe magnitude in [1, sample_max], proven above
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < count) : (i += 1) {
        const xl: i16 = sc.left.buf[start + i];
        const xr: i16 = sc.right.buf[start + i];
        const ml: i64 = if (xl < 0) -@as(i64, xl) else @as(i64, xl); // |left| — safe in i64 (|sample_min| = 32768 fits)
        const mr: i64 = if (xr < 0) -@as(i64, xr) else @as(i64, xr); // |right|
        // The LINKED detector: the louder of the two channels is the master magnitude. Above the ceiling it names the one gain both channels share; within the ceiling the pair is left whole.
        const key: i64 = if (ml >= mr) ml else mr;
        if (key > ceiling) {
            // ONE shared gain ceil/key across both channels -- the ratio->∞ compressor. The peak-holding channel (|x| == key) lands exactly on ceil (key-ceil/key = ceil); the quieter channel lands proportionally below it,
            // so nothing crosses the ceiling and the L:R ratio holds. |x|-ceil <= 32767-32767 fits i64; the quotient is <= |x| since ceil < key here, so it fits i16 and never flips sign.
            const nl: i64 = @divTrunc(ml * ceiling, key);
            const nr: i64 = @divTrunc(mr * ceiling, key);
            // invariant: the shared gain reduces to at most the ceiling and never below zero, on each channel.
            assert(nl <= ml and nl >= 0 and nl <= ceiling and nr <= mr and nr >= 0 and nr <= ceiling);
            const yl: i16 = @intCast(if (xl < 0) -nl else nl); // safe: 0 <= nl <= ceiling <= sample_max
            const yr: i16 = @intCast(if (xr < 0) -nr else nr);
            sc.left.buf[start + i] = yl;
            sc.right.buf[start + i] = yr;
            // invariant: each written sample never exceeds the ceiling in magnitude, and its sign matches the input's.
            assert(yl <= ceiling and yl >= -ceiling and yr <= ceiling and yr >= -ceiling);
            assert((xl >= 0 and yl >= 0) or (xl <= 0 and yl <= 0));
            assert((xr >= 0 and yr >= 0) or (xr <= 0 and yr <= 0));
        }
        // key <= ceiling: the pair is already within the ceiling -- both samples are left exactly as they were.
    }

    // invariant: a limiter changes sample values only -- each channel holds its starting length and the channels leave balanced, the stereo image aligned in time.
    assert(sc.left.len == len and sc.right.len == len);
    assert(sc.left.len <= sc.left.buf.len and sc.right.len <= sc.right.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE BRICKWALL + LINKED-GAIN LAW -- no sample exceeds ceil, the peak-holding channel equals ALES49's mono limit byte for byte, both channels scaled by the same fraction, the independent lift differing;
//     THE IMAGE LAW -- no magnitude expands, no sign flips, an out-of-phase over-ceiling pair returns still out of phase; THE SUB-CEILING LAW -- a pair within the linked ceiling passes byte for byte on both; THE
//     CEILING-AT-MAX LAW -- a ceiling at sample_max is the identity for all in-range audio; THE BALANCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW -- length held, each refusal leaving both channels untouched and balanced, count = 0
//     the identity on both -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

fn abs16(v: i16) i32 {
    return if (v < 0) -@as(i32, v) else @as(i32, v);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. THE BRICKWALL + LINKED-GAIN LAW -- the stereo crux. A master where the LEFT always holds the peak (|left| >= |right| everywhere), ceil 1000: no sample in either channel exceeds 1000; the loud left equals ALES49's
    //    mono limit(left) byte for byte, and the right is scaled by the SAME fraction ceil/key -- a different, image-preserving result than limiting the right on its own.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 8000, -4000, 500, -2000 }; // index 2 within the ceiling on both — that pair passes
        const rr = [_]i16{ 2000, -1000, 100, -1500 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_limit(&sc, 0, 4, 1000);

        // brickwall: nothing crosses the ceiling on either channel.
        for (sc.left.samples()) |s| if (abs16(s) > 1000) return error.LeftCrossedCeiling;
        for (sc.right.samples()) |s| if (abs16(s) > 1000) return error.RightCrossedCeiling;

        // the loud left equals ALES49's mono limit of it, proven against the mono tool directly.
        var mono_l = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mono_l, 0, &ll);
        try limit.limit(&mono_l, 0, 4, 1000);
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), mono_l.samples())) return error.PeakLeftNotMono;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1000, -1000, 500, -1000 })) return error.PeakLeftHandWrong;

        // the right scaled by the SAME fraction: idx0 key8000 -> 2000-1000/8000 = 250; idx1 key4000 -> 1000-1000/4000 = 250; idx2 key500<=ceil -> pass 100; idx3 key2000 -> 1500-1000/2000 = 750.
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 250, -250, 100, -750 })) return error.LinkedRightWrong;

        // the INDEPENDENT lift genuinely differs -- mono limit of the right alone would pin only its over-ceiling samples, leaving the image shifted.
        var mono_r = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mono_r, 0, &rr);
        try limit.limit(&mono_r, 0, 4, 1000); // 2000→1000, -1000 within→-1000, 100→100, -1500→-1000
        if (eq(sc.right.samples(), mono_r.samples())) return error.IndependentDidNotDiffer;
        if (!eq(mono_r.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1000, -1000, 100, -1000 })) return error.IndependentLimitWrong;
    }

    // 2. THE IMAGE LAW -- the shared gain preserves the L:R ratio. Over a mixed master no output magnitude exceeds its input's and no sign flips; and an out-of-phase over-ceiling pair (right = −left) comes back STILL out
    //    of phase, both scaled by the identical fraction (both landing on the ceiling with opposite sign where they hold the peak together).
    {
        const il = [_]i16{ 8000, -4000, 900, -6000 };
        const ir = [_]i16{ -3000, 2000, 400, -1500 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &il, &ir);
        try stereo_limit(&sc, 0, 4, 1000);
        const l = sc.left.samples();
        const r = sc.right.samples();
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < 4) : (i += 1) {
            if (abs16(l[i]) > abs16(il[i]) or abs16(r[i]) > abs16(ir[i])) return error.MagnitudeExpanded;
            if ((il[i] > 0 and l[i] < 0) or (il[i] < 0 and l[i] > 0)) return error.LeftSignFlipped;
            if ((ir[i] > 0 and r[i] < 0) or (ir[i] < 0 and r[i] > 0)) return error.RightSignFlipped;
        }

        var op = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&op, &[_]i16{ 8000, -4000, 2000 }, &[_]i16{ -8000, 4000, -2000 });
        try stereo_limit(&op, 0, 3, 1000);
        const ol = op.left.samples();
        const orr = op.right.samples();
        var j: u32 = 0;
        while (j < 3) : (j += 1) {
            if (@as(i32, orr[j]) != -@as(i32, ol[j])) return error.OutOfPhaseNotPreserved;
        }
        // equal-magnitude out-of-phase pairs both hold the peak, so both land exactly on the ceiling.
        if (!eq(ol, &[_]i16{ 1000, -1000, 1000 })) return error.OutOfPhaseMagnitudeWrong;
    }

    // 3. THE SUB-CEILING LAW -- a master whose every linked key is within the ceiling passes byte for byte on both (below the linked ceiling is the identity).
    {
        const ql = [_]i16{ 800, -600, 200, -900 };
        const qr = [_]i16{ -400, 300, -700, 500 }; // every |sample| <= 900 < 1000, so every key <= ceiling
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &ql, &qr);
        try stereo_limit(&sc, 0, 4, 1000);
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &ql) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &qr)) return error.SubCeilingNotIdentity;
    }

    // 4. THE CEILING-AT-MAX LAW -- a ceiling at sample_max is the identity for all in-range audio, since every magnitude is within it, so every linked key is too.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 32767, -32767, 100, -20000 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ -30000, 15000, 400, 32767 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_limit(&sc, 0, 4, @as(i32, timeline.sample_max));
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &ll) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &rr)) return error.CeilAtMaxNotIdentity;
    }

    // 5a. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW -- the channels stay equal length and each holds its starting length; a limiter writes values only.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 8000, -4000, 200, -9000, 100 }, &[_]i16{ -400, 3000, -700, 5000, -50 });
        try stereo_limit(&sc, 0, 5, 1000);
        if (sc.left.len != sc.right.len) return error.Unbalanced;
        if (sc.left.len != 5) return error.LengthChanged;
    }

    // 5b. THE ATOMICITY LAW -- a zero ceiling, an over-rail ceiling, and an out-of-range span each refuse by name with BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced.
    {
        const tl = [_]i16{ 5, -6, 7 };
        const tr = [_]i16{ -7, 6, -5 };

        var a = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&a, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_limit(&a, 0, 3, 0)) |_| return error.ZeroCeilSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCeiling) return error.WrongZeroCeil;
        if (!eq(a.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(a.right.samples(), &tr) or a.left.len != a.right.len) return error.ZeroCeilRefusalMutated;

        var b = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&b, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_limit(&b, 0, 3, @as(i32, timeline.sample_max) + 1)) |_| return error.OverRailSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCeiling) return error.WrongOverRail;
        if (!eq(b.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(b.right.samples(), &tr) or b.left.len != b.right.len) return error.OverRailRefusalMutated;

        var c = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&c, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_limit(&c, 1, 3, 1000)) |_| return error.BadRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongBadRange;
        if (!eq(c.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(c.right.samples(), &tr) or c.left.len != c.right.len) return error.BadRangeRefusalMutated;
    }

    // 5c. THE DEGENERATE LAW -- count = 0 (an empty span in the middle) is the identity on both channels; no pair to limit.
    {
        var noop = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&noop, &[_]i16{ 8000, -8000, 4000 }, &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 });
        try stereo_limit(&noop, 1, 0, 1000); // empty span — identity
        if (!eq(noop.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 8000, -8000, 4000 })) return error.ZeroCountLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(noop.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 })) return error.ZeroCountRightChanged;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-limit: the BRICKWALL LIMITER CARRIED INTO STEREO, the ratio→∞ corner of the linked gain — both a true ceiling and an image held whole. Limit a span of sample-PAIRS in a master to a magnitude " ++
        "ceiling, the detector key per pair being max(|left|, |right|), the louder of the two. ALES49 named the mono limiter: a sample within the ceiling passes byte for byte, a sample louder is pinned to sign(x)·ceil. " ++
        "Lifting it per channel would flatten an asymmetric transient on the loud channel while the quiet passes, shifting the image, so this rung takes ALES157's linked gain to its LIMIT: one gain from the linked " ++
        "detector, applied to both. The quiet beauty is that the linked form is SIMULTANEOUSLY a true brickwall (no written sample exceeds the ceiling) AND image-preserving (both channels share one gain ceil/key). THE " ++
        "BRICKWALL + LINKED-GAIN LAW (the crux): after the call no sample in either channel exceeds the ceiling, and the peak-holding channel equals ALES49's mono limit byte for byte (a left-always-peak master has left' " ++
        "== mono limit(left)) while both channels are scaled by the same fraction — the independent lift genuinely differs. THE IMAGE LAW: the shared gain preserves the L:R ratio, an out-of-phase over-ceiling pair " ++
        "returns still out of phase, no magnitude expands, no sign flips. THE SUB-CEILING LAW: a pair within the linked ceiling passes byte for byte on both. THE CEILING-AT-MAX LAW: a ceiling at sample_max is the " ++
        "identity for all in-range audio. THE BALANCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: left.len == right.len after, a zero or over-rail ceiling and an out-of-range span each refusing by name with both channels untouched and " ++
        "balanced, count = 0 the identity on both. Purely local — two bounded i16 Clips limited by one linked gain, one comparison and at most one multiply-then-divide per channel per sample (in i64, no overflow), " ++
        "memoryless, never a length; no real sample rate, no anti-aliasing, no network, no keys, no funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
